/**
 * @mainpage libweif library manual
 *
 * Welcome to the documentation for the `libweif` library — a C++ library for calculating weight functions for optical turbulence in the atmosphere.
 *
 * The present documentation is arranged in the following sections:
 * - @subpage tutorial — Practical examples demonstrating how to use the library
 * - @subpage howto — Building and installation guides
 * - @subpage api_reference
 * - @subpage internals — library internals and explanations
 */

/**
 * @page tutorial Tutorial
 *
 * @tableofcontents
 *
 * - @subpage tutorial_spectral_filter
 * - @subpage tutorial_aperture_filter
 *
 */

/**
 * @page howto How-to guides
 *
 * @tableofcontents
 *
 * @section installation Installation
 *
 * Currently, there is only one way to install `libweif` — build from source code.
 *
 * @subsection source_build Build from sources
 *
 * The source code is located at [GitHub](https://github.com/matwey/libweif).
 * Use the [GitHub Releases page](https://github.com/matwey/libweif/releases) to obtain the latest stable release of the library.
 * To get the development version, run:
 * ```bash
 * git clone https://github.com/matwey/libweif
 * ```
 *
 * Only Linux is supported at the moment. To build the library, the following
 * dependencies are required:
 * - C++ compiler with C++20 support (gcc 10+ or clang 10+)
 * - [CMake](https://cmake.org/) build system
 * - [Boost.math](https://www.boost.org/) and [Boost.Program_options](https://www.boost.org/) libraries
 * - [FFTW3](https://www.fftw.org/) library
 * - [RapidCSV](https://github.com/d99kris/rapidcsv) library
 * - [XTensor](https://github.com/xtensor-stack/xtensor) library 0.26+
 * - [CPPUnit](https://freedesktop.org/wiki/Software/cppunit) unit testing framework
 *
 * Run the appropriate command for your Linux distribution to install the dependencies:
 * - openSUSE:
 *   ```bash
 *   sudo zypper install cmake cppunit-devel fftw3-devel gcc-c++ libboost_math-devel libboost_program_options-devel rapidcsv-devel xtensor-devel
 *   ```
 * - Ubuntu:
 *   ```bash
 *   sudo apt install cmake libcppunit-dev libfftw3-dev g++ libboost-math-dev libboost-program-options-dev rapidcsv-dev xtensor-dev
 *   ```
 * - There is also a `vcpkg.json` file for [vcpkg](https://vcpkg.io) package manager
 *   users. Before proceeding, make sure that the `CMAKE_TOOLCHAIN_FILE`
 *   environment variable stores the appropriate path to `vcpkg.cmake` for your
 *   vcpkg installation.
 *
 * Run the following commands to compile the library:
 * ```bash
 * cmake -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release -B build .
 * cmake --build build
 * ```
 *
 * Run unit tests to validate that the compiled library is consistent:
 * ```bash
 * ctest --test-dir build
 * ```
 * If the test succeeds, you should see a number of `Passed` lines.
 *
 * The final optional step is to install the library system-wide:
 * ```bash
 * sudo cmake --install build
 * ```
 * @section examples Running examples
 *
 * The library includes several example programs that demonstrate typical usage.
 * By default, examples are built together with the library. If you wish to disable
 * building examples, pass `-DBUILD_EXAMPLES:BOOL=OFF` to the CMake configuration step.
 *
 * After a successful build, the example executables can be found in the
 * `build/examples/` directory (or the corresponding build directory you specified).
 * For instance, to run the weight function example:
 *
 * ```bash
 * ./build/examples/weight_function
 * ```
 *
 * Each example may accept command-line arguments; use `--help` to see available options.
 * Some examples require input files (e.g., spectral response curves). Refer to the
 * source code of each example for details on expected file formats.
 *
 * @section usage Import the library into your project
 *
 * Once installed, `libweif` can be used in your own C++ projects. The library provides a
 * pkg‑config file (`weif.pc`) that simplifies compiler and linker flag generation.
 *
 * @subsection cmake Using CMake
 * If your project uses CMake, you can locate `libweif` via `pkg‑config`:
 * ```cmake
 * find_package(PkgConfig REQUIRED)
 * pkg_check_modules(weif REQUIRED IMPORTED_TARGET weif)
 * # ...
 * target_link_libraries(your_target PkgConfig::weif)
 * ```
 *
 * @subsection make Using Makefile
 * With `pkg‑config` you can obtain the necessary flags:
 * ```make
 * CXXFLAGS += $(shell pkg-config --cflags weif)
 * LDFLAGS  += $(shell pkg-config --libs weif)
 * ```
 * Then compile your source files with `$(CXXFLAGS)` and link with `$(LDFLAGS)`.
 *
 * @note The library depends on FFTW3, Boost.math, XTensor, and RapidCSV.
 * Ensure these dependencies are available in your build environment.
 *
 * @section contributing Contributing
 *
 * You are welcome to report the issues on [GitHub Issues page](https://github.com/matwey/libweif/issues).
 * Pull requests are also accepted on GitHub.
 *
 */

